              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.11: Constructing irrational lengths and locating them on the number line"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.38,
            0.399,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.419,
            0.855,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Try to extend this method for constructing line segments of lengths "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " using a ruler and a compass. Generalise this method to construct a line segment of any length of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , where n is a positive integer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.514,
            0.796,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.5.3 The Story of Pi (π) and Madhava’s Infinite Series"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.543,
            0.886,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Another famous irrational number is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its diameter. For centuries, mathematicians sought better and better fractional approximations for π. Āryabhaṭa (499 CE) gave the highly accurate approximation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{3927}{1250} = 3.1416"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , but stated that this was only an asanna (approximation), and indicated that an exact fraction could likely not be found."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.677,
            0.886,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, because π is irrational (as was formally proven by Lambert in 1761), no single fraction (or finitely many fractions) can ever provide a perfect formula for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\pi ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " An exact formula for π was first unlocked in the 14th century by "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mādhava of Sangamagrama",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", who launched the Kerala School of Mathematics. Mādhava realised that to express an irrational number, you cannot use a single fraction; you must use an infinite sum. He discovered the profound infinite series:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.357,
            0.822,
            0.638,
            0.866
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\pi = 4 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7} + \\cdots\\right)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.867,
            0.886,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We know what it means to add 2, 100, or even a lakh terms. What does it mean to add an infinite number of terms? It means finding the"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
